  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 216 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nTo start the long term period, Southeastern Wyoming and Western \nNebraska will remain in a southwesterly flow pattern, keeping a \ncontinued influx of monsoonal moisture on the western periphery of a \n597 dam ridge centered over the Mid-Mississippi Valley. With PWAT \nremaining at or above the 90th percentile for this time of year \n(around 0.7\"-0.9\" inches for SE Wyoming and 0.9\"-1.1\" for the \nNebraska Panhandle), it will be a matter of how much moisture we can \nactually bring to the surface and give us some much needed rainfall. \nThere is some weak isentropic ascent overnight Thursday into Friday, \nmainly over SE Wyoming, however the main shortwave trough looks to \narrive over our area earlier on Thursday when precipitation is \nexpected to most widespread. Long-range ensemble guidance hints at \nnon-zero CAPE overnight, but generally under 500 J/Kg, so a few \nshowers and non-severe thunderstorms can't be ruled out with the \nwarm advection regime in place.\n\nOn Friday, mid-level flow shifts to more WSW, which will begin to \nshut off our source for monsoonal moisture. With some remaining \nmoisture trapped underneath the ridge (i.e. PWAT still ranging from \nthe 60th to 70th percentile) combined with another subtle shortwave \ntrough that skirts across Wyoming and Nebraska Friday evening, \ncannot rule out another round of showers and storms on Friday \nafternoon and evening. Although LREF guidance has median surface \ndewpoints in the mid to upper 50s across the Panhandle, deep-layer \nshear is only forecast to be 20-25 knots, which should minimized the \nthreat of any organized severe weather. At this time, hydrological \nimpacts/localized flash flooding will be the primary concern, hence \nWPC placing our region in another marginal risk for excessive \nrainfall on Friday. Coverage is expected to be more scattered and \nlower in coverage relative to Thursday, so with less cloud cover we \ncan also expect highs closer to 80 degrees east of the I-25 \ncorridor, with low to mid 70s to the west. \n\nOn Saturday, another weak open wave will translate across SE Wyoming \nand W Nebraska. With PWAT still ranging from the the-the \npercentile, primarily along the I-80 corridor, can still expect a \nfew showers and thunderstorms later in the day, but nothing appears \nto be widespread at this time. LREF guidance only shows us at a 20-\n40% chance of receiving measurable rainfall during this period,\nso not exactly enthusiastic that we will see much of any \nprecipitation that we are desperately in need of. Temperatures \non Saturday will be slightly cooler in the 70s across the \nNebraska Panhandle, and mid to upper 60s for Southeastern \nWyoming. \n\nA shortwave ridge begins to build in to our west on Sunday, placing \nus in more of a NW flow pattern. Not only will this decrease our \noverall moisture, but with the passage of a weak front sometime \nduring the day, we can expect a slight cooling trend into early next \nweek. An open wave will remain situated to our south and west over \nCalifornia into early next week which will eventually become cut off \nfrom the polar jet to our north, placing us in more zonal flow. This \nwill translate to a dry pattern with high temperatures sitting right \naround climatology (high temperatures in Cheyenne average in the low \nto mid-70s next week). It's too far out to discuss specifics at this \nrange, but ensemble guidance suggests another longwave ridge \nbuilding across the Rocky Mountain West and Northern Great Plains \ntowards the middle of next week, which would bring a warming trend \nto our region.",
